# 1. Primary Objective

Finalize the official master registry of exactly 29 dedicated business-category AI agents for GeFlow AI.

Each agent must represent one specific business category and have its own:

- Unique agent identity.
- Permanent agent ID.
- Official agent name.
- Assigned business category.
- Business-specific mission.
- Operational responsibilities.
- Functional scope.
- Business knowledge requirements.
- Supported workflows.
- Authorized data domains.
- Required tools and capabilities.
- Read permissions.
- Write permissions.
- Prohibited operations.
- Approval requirements.
- Security boundaries.
- Performance expectations.
- Testing requirements.
- Version and lifecycle status.

The registry will become the authoritative reference for all subsequent agent architecture and implementation phases.

The objective is not to create 29 generic AI chatbots.

The objective is to establish 29 specialized business intelligence and operational agents that understand the specific workflows, inventory characteristics, business terminology, operational requirements, and decision-making needs of their assigned business categories.

**One agent = One dedicated business category.**

No category may have two different primary agents in this master registry, and no category may be left without its assigned agent.

# 2. Non-Negotiable Architecture Rules

## 2.1 Exactly 29 Dedicated Category Agents

The master registry must contain exactly 29 agent definitions.

- First agent: AGT-001.
- Last agent: AGT-029.
- Every ID must be unique.
- Every category must have exactly one assigned primary agent.
- Every agent must map to exactly one business category.
- No additional category agents may be introduced without an explicitly authorized registry revision.

## 2.2 Independent Agent Identity

Each agent must have an independent logical identity.

For example:

- AGT-001 is not AGT-002.
- AGT-018 is not AGT-019.
- AGT-029 is not a general-purpose pharmacy chatbot.

Each agent must have its own configuration, role definition, category-specific instructions, operational scope, and permission profile.

Agents may share infrastructure, model providers, monitoring services, and common technical utilities.

Sharing infrastructure does not mean sharing agent identity or unrestricted access.

## 2.3 Same-Category Business Isolation

A single category-agent definition may serve multiple businesses belonging to its assigned category.

However, each business must have a separate execution context.

For example:

- Business A uses the Restaurant Agent.
- Business B also uses the Restaurant Agent.
- Business C also uses the Restaurant Agent.

All three businesses use the same Restaurant Agent definition, but their runtime contexts must remain isolated.

Each business must have separate:

- Business data.
- User permissions.
- Conversation history.
- Agent memory.
- Business preferences.
- Operational context.
- Tool authorization.
- Workflow state.
- Reports and generated insights.
- Audit records.

The Restaurant Agent must never expose Business A's information to Business B or Business C.

This isolation must be enforced through trusted server-side authorization and tenant-scoped data access, not merely through AI prompt instructions.

## 2.4 No Default Cross-Agent Delegation

Agents must not independently delegate tasks to other category agents.

Examples:

- Restaurant Agent cannot directly execute Pharmacy Agent workflows.
- Grocery Agent cannot directly execute Vehicle Repair Agent workflows.
- Bakery Agent cannot independently access Jewelry Agent operations.

A central orchestration layer may later identify the appropriate category agent for a request and route the request through authorized execution.

That routing does not grant the requesting agent unrestricted access to another agent's identity, memory, tools, or permissions.

Cross-agent delegation remains prohibited by default unless a future architecture decision explicitly authorizes a controlled exception.

## 2.5 Existing GeFlow Remains the Source of Truth

Agents must operate on the existing GeFlow application and its authorized services.

They must not create competing copies of:

- POS systems.
- Inventory systems.
- Purchase systems.
- Supplier databases.
- Customer databases.
- Business records.
- Financial ledgers.
- Reporting databases.

Agents may interpret, analyze, and operate on existing GeFlow data through authorized interfaces.

The registry must not assume that a new database or API is required for every agent.

## 2.6 Agents Are Functional Roles, Not Necessarily Separate Models

The registry defines logical agent identities and their responsibilities.

It does not require:

- 29 separate AI model providers.
- 29 independently trained foundation models.
- 29 separate servers.
- 29 separate databases.
- 29 duplicated application architectures.

A shared AI execution infrastructure may serve multiple agents, provided the correct identity, context, configuration, tools, permissions, and data boundaries are enforced for every execution.

